    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stetson/jobs/5422629/director-of-global-initiatives-ex9874": {
      "title": "Director of Global Initiatives",
      "description": "Stetson University - Career Opportunities | . For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. \n        Here are the instructions how to enable JavaScript in your web browser Director of Global Initiatives  (EX9874) | . page has loaded. Director of Global Initiatives  (EX9874) | . page has loaded. Click here or press any key to continue. Stetson University - Career Opportunities Menu Stetson University - Career Opportunities Sign In Don't have an account? Create one . Already have an account? Sign In . User profile dropdown menu for Username Applications Inbox Profile Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Applications Inbox Profile Go to Background Investigation Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Welcome! Thanks for creating an account. . powered by NEOGOV ® Need this page in another language? You can translate this page using your browser's built-in translation feature. Tip: In Chrome or Edge, right-click anywhere on the page and select \"Translate to [Language].\" Director of Global Initiatives  (EX9874) Print Apply ﻿ Director of Global Initiatives  (EX9874) Salary Depends on Qualifications Location DeLand, FL Job Type Full-Time Staff Division Academic Affairs Department WORLD - International Learning Opening Date 07/24/2026 FLSA Exempt Bargaining Unit N/A -- Pay Grade: 09 Description Benefits Job Description The Director of Global Initiatives provides strategic leadership for the institution's global engagement initiatives. Reporting to the Office of the Provost and Academic Affairs, the Director leads the development, implementation, and assessment of comprehensive international education programs, including education abroad, international student and scholar services, global partnerships, and campus internationalization. The position advances the University's global mission through collaboration with faculty, staff, students, alumni, donors, and international partners. Essential Job Functions and Responsibilities: Employment duties include, but are not limited to: Strategic Leadership: Provides strategic counsel to the Provost and to Academic Affairs on issues of campus internationalization to implement collaborative initiatives that strengthen the institution’s global presence and engagement. Advise the Provost and Academic Affairs on institutional strategies and policies related to international education and global engagement. Provide strategic leadership for study abroad, international student and scholar services, international partnerships, and campus internationalization initiatives. Collaborate across academic and administrative units to integrate global learning throughout the student experience and curriculum. Develop and implement long-range strategic initiatives that expand international learning opportunities and institutional global engagement. Global Partnerships and International Programs: Lead the cultivation, negotiation, and management of strategic global partnerships that support student and faculty exchanges, collaborative research, and academic initiatives with legal counsel. Establish, cultivate, and maintain strategic partnerships with universities and organizations worldwide to support student and faculty exchange, collaborative research, and academic programming. Negotiate, develop, and oversee Memoranda of Understanding (MOUs) and partnership agreements in collaboration with the University's external legal counsel. Direct the University's international exchange programs, including the recruitment, orientation, and support of J-1 exchange students, scholars, interns, and visiting faculty. Education Abroad Leadership: Provide strategic leadership for operational policies and compliance of all student and faculty education abroad programs and experiences. Provide management protocols for international travel and education abroad programs. Lead the creation and expansion of innovative global learning initiatives designed to increase student participation and access. Foster faculty engagement through professional development initiatives that support the design and implementation of high-quality faculty-led study abroad programs. Recruit and provide oversight of the first-year study abroad programs such as the Global Gateways Program currently in Dublin, Ireland. Faculty Development and Campus Internationalization Develop and administer faculty development opportunities focused on international education, including seminars, workshops, travel grants, and professional learning communities. Collaborate with faculty to integrate global learning outcomes into academic programs and curricula. Serve on institutional committees responsible for international learning policies, curricular innovation, academic advising, and global engagement initiatives. Student Success and Compliance Serve as a university Designated School Official (DSO) for F-1 students and Responsible Officer (RO) for J-1 exchange visitors, ensuring compliance with all federal immigration regulations and SEVIS reporting requirements. Provide leadership for international student success initiatives that promote recruitment, retention, academic achievement, and campus engagement. Respond to and manage student, scholar, and education abroad emergencies on a 24/7 on-call basis. Budget, Advancement, and Administration: Provide strategic leadership and oversight for international education professional staff, departmental budgets, and collaborative resource-raising. Develop and manage departmental operating budgets, endowed funds, and financial resources supporting international education initiatives. Prepare reports and assessments for senior leadership, donors, and the Board of Trustees demonstrating program effectiveness and strategic impact. Partner with University Advancement to identify funding opportunities, prepare donor proposals, and secure philanthropic support for international initiatives. Supervise professional staff within WORLD: The David and Leighan Rinker Center for International Learning and provide leadership for departmental planning, assessment, and operations. Institutional Service: Provide leadership and counsel to institutional committees and professional organizations that influence the strategic direction of international learning. Serve on university-wide committees supporting international education, academic advising, student success, and institutional planning. Participate in institutional emergency management and crisis response efforts related to international students, scholars, and education abroad programs. Represent the University in regional, national, and international professional organizations and partnerships. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: The knowledge, skills, and abilities for this role include: Comprehensive knowledge of international education best practices, including education abroad program design, international student and scholar services, and global partnership development. Working knowledge of federal immigration regulations governing F-1 and J-1 visa holders, including SEVIS reporting and compliance requirements. Demonstrated skill in strategic planning and the ability to translate institutional priorities into actionable global engagement initiatives. Strong financial acumen, including experience developing and managing operating budgets, endowed funds, and grant or donor-funded accounts. Ability to supervise, mentor, and develop professional staff in a fast-paced, service-oriented environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to represent the University persuasively to faculty, students, donors, and international partners. Skill in negotiating and administering international agreements (MOUs) in coordination with legal counsel. Ability to exercise sound judgment and make timely decisions during student or program emergencies, including outside of normal business hours. Cultural competence and demonstrated ability to work effectively with individuals from diverse national, cultural, and linguistic backgrounds. Ability to analyze program data and prepare clear, compelling reports for senior leadership and the Board of Trustees. Proficiency with relevant software systems (e.g., SEVIS, study abroad management platforms, database and reporting tools). Commitment to supporting the University's Mission and Values. Required Qualifications: This position requires: Master's degree required At least five (5) years of progressive leadership experience in international education, higher education administration, or a related field. Demonstrated expertise in education abroad, international student and scholar services, immigration compliance (F-1/J-1), and global partnership development. Experience managing budgets, supervising professional staff, and leading strategic initiatives. Proven success in fundraising, grant development, and donor relations preferred. Strong interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills with the ability to collaborate across diverse constituencies. Demonstrated commitment to global learning, intercultural competence, student success, and inclusive excellence. Ideal Candidate Qualifications: The ideal candidate’s qualifications include: Doctoral or terminal degree in international education, higher education administration, or a related field. Seven-plus (7+) years of progressive leadership experience in international education, with direct oversight of both education abroad and international student/scholar services functions. Prior experience serving as a Designated School Official (DSO) and/or Responsible Officer (RO). Established international partnership network or prior experience developing partnerships in one or more target regions. Fluency or proficiency in a second language. Experience living, studying, or working abroad. Demonstrated success securing philanthropic or grant support for international programming. Experience with a private, residential liberal arts institution or comparable university setting. Familiarity with study abroad risk management and international crisis response protocols. Work Environment: Primarily an indoor office environment with standard lighting, temperature, and noise levels. Some regional, national, and international travel are required, including occasional travel to partner institutions and program sites abroad. Availability required outside of standard business hours to respond to student and program emergencies (24/7 on-call responsibilities as needed). Fast-paced environment with competing priorities and periodic seasonal demands (e.g., peak study abroad application and departure cycles). Flexible Work Arrangement (FWA) may be available in accordance with University policy. Pay Grade: 9 Work authorization sponsorship may be available for this position. However, sponsorship is contingent upon the non-applicability of the visa petition fees outlined in the relevant Executive Order signed September 19, 2025. Due to the recent federally-mandated increased financial expense, the university is not in a position to absorb this significantly increased cost of sponsorship for new H-1B petitions at this time. International candidates are encouraged to apply, but should be aware that eligibility for sponsorship will be evaluated in accordance with current U.S. immigration regulations and institutional policies. This position is benefit eligible. Stetson University offers a variety of benefits for our full-time employees including comprehensive Medical, Dental, and Vision insurance, employer paid life and disability insurance, generous leave and holiday benefits, employer retirement contributions, tuition waivers for employees and eligible dependents, and others. All employees are also eligible to enjoy our Hatter Perks which include a variety of discounts and special privileges within our community. Please visit our employee benefits webpage for more information. Employer Stetson University Division Academic Affairs Address 421 N. Woodland Blvd. DeLand, Florida, 32723 Phone 386-822-8710 Website https://www.stetson.edu/ Apply Please verify your email address Verify Email Stetson University - Career Opportunities Account Privacy Policy Terms Of Use Accessibility Statement Privacy Settings Copyright © 2026 Do not sell my personal information × For California residents, and to the extent NEOGOV sells your personal information as the term “sell” is defined under the California Consumer Privacy Act, you have the right to opt-out of the sale of your personal information by us to third parties at any time. This setting only applies to registered account holders that have opted-in for an Access Membership. If you wish to opt-out click follow these steps: Log into your Job Seeker Profile Click your name in the upper right-hand corner Select Account Settings Select the Preferences section Click the Edit button next to Access Membership Switch Toggle to off Click Save If you do not have a registered account this setting does not apply, however, you can review your right on our privacy policy . If you require further assistance, please email privacy@neogov.com × Accessibility Statement At Governmentjobs.com, Inc. d/b/a NEOGOV, we recognize the importance of making our products\n                    and services accessible to all individuals, including those with disabilities, and are continually\n                    working to improve the experience for all users. Commitment to Accessibility We are committed to following best practices, including the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n                    (WCAG) 2.1, Level AA, to the extent feasible, however, we do not warrant compliance with these\n                    standards or any other accessibility standards. We are continually enhancing our platforms to\n                    